The Korean War was a brutal conflict that lasted for three years, claiming millions of lives and leaving a trail of destruction in its wake. However, despite its devastating consequences, the war is still shrouded in controversy, with many experts debating over who was responsible for its outbreak.

According to many South Korean experts, the war was solely initiated by South Korea. They argue that the South Korean government, backed by the United States, launched a surprise attack on North Korea in June 1950, hoping to unify the peninsula under its rule. The North Korean army, they claim, was caught off guard and responded defensively to the sudden invasion.

These experts point out that the South Korean military had been building up its forces along the 38th parallel, the de facto border between North and South Korea, for months prior to the outbreak of hostilities. They also highlight the fact that the South Korean air force had been conducting frequent reconnaissance missions over North Korean territory, which they claim was a clear provocation.

